    <header class="hero">
      <div class="kicker">Student Reference · ASP.NET Core</div>
      <h1>Routing &amp; Validation, stop by stop</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A request enters your app as a raw URL and leaves as a validated, bound, versioned response.
        This guide walks that journey in order — how the framework <em>finds</em> the right code to run,
        how it <em>fills in</em> that code's parameters, and how it <em>checks</em> the data before trusting it.
      </p>
      <div class="route-strip">
        <span class="node">URL</span><span class="seg"></span>
        <span class="node">Routing</span><span class="seg"></span>
        <span class="node">Constraints</span><span class="seg"></span>
        <span class="node">Model Binding</span><span class="seg"></span>
        <span class="node">Versioning</span><span class="seg"></span>
        <span class="node">Validation</span><span class="seg"></span>
        <span class="node">Action Runs</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="content">

      <!-- ===================== 1. INTRO TO ROUTING ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="routing-intro">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">1</span>Routing</div>
        <h2>Intro: Routing</h2>
        <p>
          Routing is the mechanism ASP.NET Core uses to match an incoming HTTP request
          (a URL plus an HTTP verb, like <code class="inline">GET /products/7</code>) to the piece of code that should
          handle it — usually a controller action or a minimal API endpoint. Without routing, the framework
          would have no way of knowing that <code class="inline">/products/7</code> should call
          <code class="inline">GetProduct(int id)</code> instead of some other method.
        </p>
        <p>Routing does three jobs at once:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Matching</strong> — compares the request's URL and method against a table of known route patterns.</li>
          <li><strong>Extraction</strong> — pulls values out of the URL (like the <code class="inline">7</code> in <code class="inline">/products/7</code>) as route data.</li>
          <li><strong>Dispatch</strong> — hands the request, along with that route data, to the matching endpoint.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In modern ASP.NET Core, routing is registered as middleware in the request pipeline, typically in <code class="inline">Program.cs</code>:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">Program.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddControllers();

<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> app = builder.Build();

app.UseRouting();        <span class="hljs-comment">// locates a matching endpoint</span>
app.UseAuthorization();  <span class="hljs-comment">// runs after the endpoint is chosen</span>
app.MapControllers();    <span class="hljs-comment">// registers attribute-routed controllers</span>

app.Run();</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <b>Why students should care</b>
          Every bug that looks like "my controller never gets hit" or "I'm getting a 404 for a route that should exist"
          traces back to routing. Understanding this stop first makes every later topic — binding, versioning,
          validation — make sense, because they all depend on the request first being routed correctly.
        </div>

        <h3>Example: how the route table picks a match</h3>
        <p>When several routes could plausibly match a URL, ASP.NET Core picks the most specific one — literal
        segments beat placeholders, and constrained placeholders beat unconstrained ones:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">example</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment">// Route table (registered in this order doesn't matter — specificity wins):</span>
[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"products/featured"</span>)</span>]      <span class="hljs-comment">// literal segment</span>
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetFeatured</span>()</span> =&gt; Ok(_repo.Featured());

[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"products/{id:int}"</span>)</span>]      <span class="hljs-comment">// constrained placeholder</span>
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetById</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> id</span>)</span> =&gt; Ok(_repo.Find(id));

<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /products/featured  -&gt; matches GetFeatured() (literal beats placeholder)</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /products/7         -&gt; matches GetById(7)</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /products/abc       -&gt; matches NEITHER route -&gt; 404</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== 2. ROUTING TYPES ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="routing-types">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">2</span>Routing</div>
        <h2>Routing Types: Attribute &amp; Conventional Routing</h2>
        <p>
          ASP.NET Core supports two styles of defining routes. You can mix both in the same project, though
          Web API projects almost always favor attribute routing.
        </p>

        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div class="compare-card a">
            <h4><span class="chip"></span>Conventional Routing</h4>
            <p>Routes are defined once, centrally, using a URL <em>pattern</em> with placeholders. The framework
            matches any URL that fits the pattern and infers the controller/action from it.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Defined centrally in <code class="inline">Program.cs</code></li>
              <li>Good fit for MVC apps with predictable, uniform URLs</li>
              <li>Harder to give one action a "special" custom URL</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="compare-card b">
            <h4><span class="chip"></span>Attribute Routing</h4>
            <p>Routes are declared directly above the controller or action using <code class="inline">[Route]</code>,
            <code class="inline">[HttpGet]</code>, etc. Each action owns its exact URL.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Defined next to the code it routes to</li>
              <li>Standard choice for Web APIs</li>
              <li>Full control over each endpoint's URL shape</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>Conventional routing example</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">Program.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">app.MapControllerRoute(
    name: <span class="hljs-string">"default"</span>,
    pattern: <span class="hljs-string">"{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}"</span>);</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>
          This single line matches <code class="inline">/Products/Details/7</code> to
          <code class="inline">ProductsController.Details(int id)</code>, because the segments line up:
          <code class="inline">controller = Products</code>, <code class="inline">action = Details</code>,
          <code class="inline">id = 7</code>. The <code class="inline">?</code> marks <code class="inline">id</code> as optional.
        </p>

        <h3>Attribute routing example</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">ProductsController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">[<span class="hljs-meta">ApiController</span>]
[<span class="hljs-meta">Route(<span class="hljs-string">"api/[controller]"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">ProductsController</span> : <span class="hljs-title">ControllerBase</span>
{
    [<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet</span>]                  <span class="hljs-comment">// GET /api/products</span>
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetAll</span>()</span> =&gt; Ok(_repo.GetAll());

    [<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"{id:int}"</span>)</span>]      <span class="hljs-comment">// GET /api/products/7</span>
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetById</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> id</span>)</span> =&gt; Ok(_repo.Find(id));

    [<span class="hljs-meta">HttpPost</span>]                 <span class="hljs-comment">// POST /api/products</span>
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">Create</span>(<span class="hljs-params">Product product</span>)</span> =&gt; Ok(_repo.Add(product));
}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <b>Rule of thumb</b>
          Use conventional routing for classic MVC apps with pages (Home/About/Contact-style URLs).
          Use attribute routing for Web APIs, where each endpoint's URL and verb need to be explicit and
          independently controllable.
        </div>

        <h3>More conventional routing: multiple patterns</h3>
        <p>A conventional-routing app can register several patterns, matched top to bottom — handy for giving
        one area of the site a different URL shape than the rest:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">Program.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">app.MapControllerRoute(
    name: <span class="hljs-string">"blog"</span>,
    pattern: <span class="hljs-string">"blog/{year}/{month}/{slug}"</span>,
    defaults: <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> { controller = <span class="hljs-string">"Blog"</span>, action = <span class="hljs-string">"Post"</span> });

app.MapControllerRoute(
    name: <span class="hljs-string">"default"</span>,
    pattern: <span class="hljs-string">"{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}"</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /blog/2026/08/routing-in-aspnet   -&gt; BlogController.Post(year, month, slug)</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /Products/Details/7               -&gt; falls through to the default route</span></code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>More attribute routing: token replacement &amp; prefixes</h3>
        <p>Attribute routing supports tokens like <code class="inline">[controller]</code> and
        <code class="inline">[action]</code>, so the route text updates automatically if you rename the class —
        and a controller-level <code class="inline">[Route]</code> acts as a shared prefix for every action inside it:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">ReviewsController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">[<span class="hljs-meta">ApiController</span>]
[<span class="hljs-meta">Route(<span class="hljs-string">"api/[controller]"</span>)</span>]           <span class="hljs-comment">// becomes "api/reviews"</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">ReviewsController</span> : <span class="hljs-title">ControllerBase</span>
{
    [<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"{id:int}"</span>)</span>]             <span class="hljs-comment">// GET api/reviews/5</span>
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">Get</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> id</span>)</span> =&gt; Ok();

    [<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"product/{productId:int}"</span>)</span>]   <span class="hljs-comment">// GET api/reviews/product/9</span>
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetForProduct</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> productId</span>)</span> =&gt; Ok();

    [<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"~/api/latest-reviews"</span>)</span>] <span class="hljs-comment">// "~/" overrides the prefix entirely</span>
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">Latest</span>()</span> =&gt; Ok();  <span class="hljs-comment">// GET api/latest-reviews</span>
}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== 3. ROUTE CONSTRAINTS ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="route-constraints">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">3</span>Routing</div>
        <h2>Route Constraints</h2>
        <p>
          A route constraint restricts what values a route parameter will accept, so that only URLs matching
          a certain shape or type reach an action. If the constraint fails, routing simply treats the URL as
          not matching that route (usually resulting in a 404, or falling through to another matching route).
        </p>
        <p>Constraints are written after the parameter name, separated by a colon: <code class="inline">{id:int}</code>.</p>

        <table class="ref">
          <tbody><tr><th>Constraint</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
          <tr><td><code>int</code></td><td><code>{id:int}</code></td><td>Value must parse as an integer</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>bool</code></td><td><code>{active:bool}</code></td><td>Value must be true/false</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>guid</code></td><td><code>{id:guid}</code></td><td>Value must be a valid GUID</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>alpha</code></td><td><code>{code:alpha}</code></td><td>Letters only (a–z, A–Z)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>minlength(n)</code></td><td><code>{name:minlength(3)}</code></td><td>String at least n characters</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>range(a,b)</code></td><td><code>{age:range(18,60)}</code></td><td>Integer within a range</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>regex(pattern)</code></td><td><code>{sku:regex(^[A-Z]{3}\d{4}$)}</code></td><td>Value must match the regular expression</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>required</code></td><td><code>{name:required}</code></td><td>Segment must be present</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>

        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">OrdersController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment">// Only matches when id is a real integer, e.g. /api/orders/42</span>
[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"{id:int}"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetOrder</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> id</span>)</span> =&gt; Ok(_repo.Find(id));

<span class="hljs-comment">// Combine constraints: int AND within a range</span>
[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"age/{age:int:range(18,60)}"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetByAge</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> age</span>)</span> =&gt; Ok();

<span class="hljs-comment">// /api/orders/abc will NOT match the route above — it 404s,</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// which is exactly what we want instead of a runtime crash</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// trying to convert "abc" to an int.</span></code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn">
          <b>Common mistake</b>
          Route constraints are for shaping which URLs match — they are <strong>not</strong> a substitute for
          validation. A constraint just filters routing; it doesn't produce friendly error messages or check
          business rules. That job belongs to Data Annotations or Fluent Validation, further down this line.
        </div>

        <h3>More examples: combining and applying constraints</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">BookingsController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment">// datetime constraint: only matches a real, parseable date segment</span>
[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"by-date/{date:datetime}"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetByDate</span>(<span class="hljs-params">DateTime date</span>)</span> =&gt; Ok();
<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /bookings/by-date/2026-08-06  -&gt; matches</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /bookings/by-date/not-a-date  -&gt; 404</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// guid constraint: perfect for resource ids that are GUIDs, not ints</span>
[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"{id:guid}"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetById</span>(<span class="hljs-params">Guid id</span>)</span> =&gt; Ok();

<span class="hljs-comment">// two placeholders, each with their own constraint</span>
[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"room/{roomCode:alpha:length(3)}/floor/{floor:int:min(1)}"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetRoom</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> roomCode, <span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> floor</span>)</span> =&gt; Ok();
<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /bookings/room/SUN/floor/4  -&gt; matches</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /bookings/room/S9/floor/4   -&gt; 404 (roomCode fails alpha + length(3))</span></code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: a custom route constraint</h3>
        <p>When the built-in constraints aren't enough, you can write your own by implementing
        <code class="inline">IRouteConstraint</code> — useful for rules like "must be a valid product SKU in our system.":</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">SkuConstraint.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">SkuConstraint</span> : <span class="hljs-title">IRouteConstraint</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">bool</span> <span class="hljs-title">Match</span>(<span class="hljs-params">HttpContext? httpContext, IRouter? route, <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> routeKey,
        RouteValueDictionary values, RouteDirection routeDirection</span>)</span>
    {
        <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">value</span> = values[routeKey]?.ToString();
        <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">value</span> != <span class="hljs-literal">null</span> &amp;&amp; Regex.IsMatch(<span class="hljs-keyword">value</span>, <span class="hljs-string">@"^[A-Z]{3}\d{4}$"</span>);
    }
}

<span class="hljs-comment">// Program.cs — register it under a friendly name</span>
builder.Services.Configure&lt;RouteOptions&gt;(options =&gt;
    options.ConstraintMap.Add(<span class="hljs-string">"sku"</span>, <span class="hljs-keyword">typeof</span>(SkuConstraint)));

<span class="hljs-comment">// Usage: [HttpGet("{sku:sku}")]  -&gt;  matches only ABC1234-style codes</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== 4. MODEL BINDING ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="model-binding">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">4</span>Binding</div>
        <h2>Model Binding</h2>
        <p>
          Once routing has picked the right action, model binding is what fills in that action's parameters
          from the incoming request. It automatically maps data from route values, the query string, form
          fields, and the request body into simple types, complex objects, and collections — so you write
          <code class="inline">GetProduct(int id)</code>, and never manually parse strings out of the request yourself.
        </p>

        <table class="ref">
          <tbody><tr><th>Source</th><th>Attribute</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Route data</td><td><code>[FromRoute]</code></td><td><code>/products/{id}</code> → <code>id</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Query string</td><td><code>[FromQuery]</code></td><td><code>?page=2&amp;size=10</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Request body</td><td><code>[FromBody]</code></td><td>JSON payload → a C# object</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Form data</td><td><code>[FromForm]</code></td><td>File uploads, HTML form posts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Headers</td><td><code>[FromHeader]</code></td><td>Custom headers like <code>X-Api-Key</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>DI container</td><td><code>[FromServices]</code></td><td>Inject a service directly into an action</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>

        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">ProductsController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"{id:int}"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetProduct</span>(<span class="hljs-params">
    [FromRoute] <span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> id,
    [FromQuery] <span class="hljs-built_in">bool</span> includeReviews</span>)</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-comment">// id comes from the URL segment, includeReviews from ?includeReviews=true</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> Ok(_repo.Find(id, includeReviews));
}

[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpPost</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">Create</span>(<span class="hljs-params">[FromBody] Product product</span>)</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-comment">// 'product' is built automatically from the posted JSON body</span>
    _repo.Add(product);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> CreatedAtAction(<span class="hljs-keyword">nameof</span>(GetProduct), <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> { id = product.Id }, product);
}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <b>Under the hood</b>
          When a parameter is a simple type (int, string, bool, Guid), the binder looks in route data and the
          query string by default. When it's a complex type (a class), the binder assumes the request body by
          default in API controllers marked <code class="inline">[ApiController]</code> — that's why
          <code class="inline">[FromBody]</code> is often optional there, but it's still good practice to be explicit.
        </div>

        <h3>Example: binding a complex object from the query string</h3>
        <p>Model binding isn't limited to simple types — it can build a whole object out of query parameters,
        which is a common pattern for search/filter endpoints:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">ProductsController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">ProductFilter</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>? Keyword { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">decimal</span>? MinPrice { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">decimal</span>? MaxPrice { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> Page { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; } = <span class="hljs-number">1</span>;
}

[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"search"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">Search</span>(<span class="hljs-params">[FromQuery] ProductFilter filter</span>)</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> Ok(_repo.Search(filter));
}

<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /api/products/search?keyword=phone&amp;minPrice=100&amp;maxPrice=500&amp;page=2</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// -&gt; filter.Keyword = "phone", filter.MinPrice = 100, filter.Page = 2</span></code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: binding collections and arrays</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">example</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"by-ids"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetMany</span>(<span class="hljs-params">[FromQuery] <span class="hljs-built_in">int</span>[] ids</span>)</span> =&gt;
    Ok(_repo.FindMany(ids));

<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /api/products/by-ids?ids=1&amp;ids=2&amp;ids=3</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// -&gt; ids = [1, 2, 3]</span>

[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpPost(<span class="hljs-string">"bulk"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">CreateMany</span>(<span class="hljs-params">[FromBody] List&lt;Product&gt; products</span>)</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-comment">// JSON body: [ { "name": "Mug" }, { "name": "Pen" } ]</span>
    _repo.AddRange(products);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> Ok(products.Count);
}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== 5. BIND ATTRIBUTES ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="bind-attribute">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">5</span>Binding</div>
        <h2>Bind Attributes</h2>
        <p>
          The <code class="inline">[Bind]</code> attribute controls exactly <em>which properties</em> of a model
          are allowed to be set during model binding. It's a safety valve against
          <strong>over-posting</strong> (also called mass assignment) — where a malicious or careless client
          sends extra fields in a request body that end up overwriting properties they shouldn't be able to touch,
          like <code class="inline">IsAdmin</code> or <code class="inline">AccountBalance</code>.
        </p>

        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">UsersController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">User</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> Id { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Name { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Email { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">bool</span> IsAdmin { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }   <span class="hljs-comment">// must NOT be settable by the client</span>
}

[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpPost</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">Register</span>(<span class="hljs-params">
    [Bind(<span class="hljs-string">"Name,Email"</span></span>)] User user)   <span class="hljs-comment">// only these two are bound</span></span>
{
    <span class="hljs-comment">// Even if the client sends { "isAdmin": true } in the JSON body,</span>
    <span class="hljs-comment">// it is silently ignored — IsAdmin keeps its default value.</span>
    _repo.Add(user);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> Ok(user);
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Bind at the parameter vs. the class</h3>
        <p><code class="inline">[Bind]</code> can also be applied directly on a model class to whitelist properties
        for every action that binds it, though scoping it per-action (as above) is usually clearer and safer.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <b>Better practice for APIs</b>
          Most modern Web API code avoids <code class="inline">[Bind]</code> altogether by using dedicated
          <strong>DTOs</strong> (e.g. <code class="inline">RegisterUserDto</code> with only <code class="inline">Name</code>
          and <code class="inline">Email</code>) instead of binding the full entity. It achieves the same protection,
          more explicitly, and keeps your domain models decoupled from your API's request shape.
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn">
          <b>Common mistake</b>
          Forgetting that <code class="inline">[Bind]</code> only restricts what can be <em>set</em> — it does not
          validate the values that are set. You still need Data Annotations or Fluent Validation on top of it.
        </div>

        <h3>Example: [Bind] applied to the class</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">User.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">[<span class="hljs-meta">Bind(<span class="hljs-string">"Name,Email,Password"</span>)</span>]   <span class="hljs-comment">// whitelist applies to EVERY action that binds User</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">User</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> Id { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Name { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Email { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Password { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">bool</span> IsAdmin { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">decimal</span> AccountBalance { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: [BindNever] and [BindRequired]</h3>
        <p>Instead of whitelisting on the action, you can blacklist a single property directly on the model —
        often clearer when only one or two fields need protecting:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">User.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">User</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> Id { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">BindRequired</span>]                 <span class="hljs-comment">// binding fails with ModelState error if missing</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Name { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">BindNever</span>]                    <span class="hljs-comment">// this property can NEVER be set via model binding</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">bool</span> IsAdmin { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">BindNever</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">decimal</span> AccountBalance { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
}

<span class="hljs-comment">// Now every action that binds User is safe automatically —</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// no need to repeat [Bind("...")] on each one.</span></code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: the DTO approach (recommended)</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">RegisterUserDto.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">RegisterUserDto</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Name { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Email { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Password { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
    <span class="hljs-comment">// IsAdmin and AccountBalance simply don't exist here — nothing to over-post</span>
}

[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpPost(<span class="hljs-string">"register"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">Register</span>(<span class="hljs-params">RegisterUserDto dto</span>)</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> user = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> User { Name = dto.Name, Email = dto.Email };
    _repo.Add(user);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> Ok(user);
}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== 6. API VERSIONING INTRO ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="versioning-intro">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">6</span>Versioning</div>
        <h2>Intro: API Versioning</h2>
        <p>
          API versioning lets you evolve an API — change response shapes, rename fields, alter behavior —
          without breaking the clients (mobile apps, other services, third parties) that already depend on the
          current version. Instead of one endpoint that changes underneath everyone, you publish
          <code class="inline">v1</code> and <code class="inline">v2</code> side by side, and let each client
          choose when to move.
        </p>
        <p>In ASP.NET Core, versioning is typically added via the <code class="inline">Asp.Versioning.Mvc</code>
        package (the modern successor to <code class="inline">Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Versioning</code>):</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">Program.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">builder.Services.AddApiVersioning(options =&gt;
{
    options.DefaultApiVersion = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> ApiVersion(<span class="hljs-number">1</span>, <span class="hljs-number">0</span>);
    options.AssumeDefaultVersionWhenUnspecified = <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>;
    options.ReportApiVersions = <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>; <span class="hljs-comment">// adds api-supported-versions header</span>
});</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <b>Why students should care</b>
          Versioning is what lets a team ship <code class="inline">v2</code> of an endpoint with breaking changes
          while <code class="inline">v1</code> keeps working exactly as before for existing clients — it's the
          difference between "we improved the API" and "we broke production for everyone still on the old version."
        </div>

        <h3>Example: one action, two versions</h3>
        <p>A single controller can expose different behavior per version by mapping the same route to different
        actions, distinguished by <code class="inline">[MapToApiVersion]</code>:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">ProductsController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">[<span class="hljs-meta">ApiVersion(<span class="hljs-string">"1.0"</span>)</span>]
[<span class="hljs-meta">ApiVersion(<span class="hljs-string">"2.0"</span>)</span>]
[<span class="hljs-meta">Route(<span class="hljs-string">"api/v{version:apiVersion}/products"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">ProductsController</span> : <span class="hljs-title">ControllerBase</span>
{
    [<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"{id:int}"</span>), MapToApiVersion(<span class="hljs-string">"1.0"</span>)</span>]
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetV1</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> id</span>)</span> =&gt;
        Ok(<span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> { id, name = _repo.Find(id).Name });          <span class="hljs-comment">// old, minimal shape</span>

    [<span class="hljs-meta">HttpGet(<span class="hljs-string">"{id:int}"</span>), MapToApiVersion(<span class="hljs-string">"2.0"</span>)</span>]
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">GetV2</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">int</span> id</span>)</span> =&gt;
        Ok(_repo.Find(id));                                   <span class="hljs-comment">// new, full object</span>
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: marking a version as deprecated</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">ProductsController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">[<span class="hljs-meta">ApiVersion(<span class="hljs-string">"1.0"</span>, Deprecated = true)</span>]   <span class="hljs-comment">// still works, but flagged to clients</span>
[<span class="hljs-meta">ApiVersion(<span class="hljs-string">"2.0"</span>)</span>]
[<span class="hljs-meta">Route(<span class="hljs-string">"api/v{version:apiVersion}/products"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">ProductsController</span> : <span class="hljs-title">ControllerBase</span> { }

<span class="hljs-comment">// Clients see this in the response headers:</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// api-supported-versions: 2.0</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// api-deprecated-versions: 1.0</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== 7. TYPES OF API VERSIONING ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="versioning-types">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">7</span>Versioning</div>
        <h2>Types of API Versioning</h2>
        <p>There are four common strategies for telling the server which version a client wants. Each has trade-offs.</p>

        <h3>1. URL Path Versioning</h3>
        <p>The version is embedded directly in the URL. Simple, visible, and cache-friendly — the most common approach.</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">ProductsController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">[<span class="hljs-meta">ApiVersion(<span class="hljs-string">"1.0"</span>)</span>]
[<span class="hljs-meta">Route(<span class="hljs-string">"api/v{version:apiVersion}/products"</span>)</span>]
<span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">ProductsController</span> : <span class="hljs-title">ControllerBase</span> { }

<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /api/v1/products</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /api/v2/products</span></code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>2. Query String Versioning</h3>
        <p>The version is passed as a query parameter, keeping the base URL unchanged.</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">example</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment">// GET /api/products?api-version=2.0</span>

builder.Services.AddApiVersioning(options =&gt;
{
    options.ApiVersionReader = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> QueryStringApiVersionReader(<span class="hljs-string">"api-version"</span>);
});</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>3. Header Versioning</h3>
        <p>The version travels in a custom HTTP header, keeping URLs completely clean.</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">example</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment">// GET /api/products</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// Header: X-Api-Version: 2.0</span>

builder.Services.AddApiVersioning(options =&gt;
{
    options.ApiVersionReader = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> HeaderApiVersionReader(<span class="hljs-string">"X-Api-Version"</span>);
});</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>4. Media Type (Accept Header) Versioning</h3>
        <p>The version is encoded in the <code class="inline">Accept</code> header's media type — the most
        "correct" from a pure REST standpoint, but the least discoverable for API consumers.</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">example</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment">// GET /api/products</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// Header: Accept: application/json;v=2.0</span>

builder.Services.AddApiVersioning(options =&gt;
{
    options.ApiVersionReader = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> MediaTypeApiVersionReader(<span class="hljs-string">"v"</span>);
});</code></pre>
        </div>

        <table class="ref">
          <tbody><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr>
          <tr><td>URL Path</td><td>Explicit, easy to test, cache-friendly</td><td>Version baked into every URL / bookmark</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Query String</td><td>Clean base URL, easy to default</td><td>Version can be dropped or forgotten by clients</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Header</td><td>Clean URLs, keeps versioning out of the resource path</td><td>Harder to test in a browser; less discoverable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Media Type</td><td>Most "RESTful"; versions content negotiation itself</td><td>Least intuitive; awkward client tooling</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>

        <h3>Example: combining strategies with a fallback</h3>
        <p>It's common to accept a version from more than one source, with a priority order — for example, prefer
        the header if present, otherwise fall back to the query string:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">Program.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">builder.Services.AddApiVersioning(options =&gt;
{
    options.ApiVersionReader = ApiVersionReader.Combine(
        <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> HeaderApiVersionReader(<span class="hljs-string">"X-Api-Version"</span>),
        <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> QueryStringApiVersionReader(<span class="hljs-string">"api-version"</span>));
});

<span class="hljs-comment">// A request with BOTH a header and a query string uses the header first.</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// GET /api/products?api-version=1.0   Header: X-Api-Version: 2.0  -&gt; resolves to 2.0</span></code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: calling each style from a client</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">client examples</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment">// URL Path</span>
GET https:<span class="hljs-comment">//api.shop.com/api/v2/products/7</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// Query String</span>
GET https:<span class="hljs-comment">//api.shop.com/api/products/7?api-version=2.0</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// Header</span>
GET https:<span class="hljs-comment">//api.shop.com/api/products/7</span>
X-Api-Version: <span class="hljs-number">2.0</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// Media Type (Accept header)</span>
GET https:<span class="hljs-comment">//api.shop.com/api/products/7</span>
Accept: application/json;v=<span class="hljs-number">2.0</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== 8. DATA ANNOTATIONS ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="data-annotations">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">8</span>Validation</div>
        <h2>Data Annotations</h2>
        <p>
          Data Annotations are attributes from <code class="inline">System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations</code>
          placed directly on model properties to declare validation rules. ASP.NET Core checks them automatically
          during model binding and populates <code class="inline">ModelState</code> with any failures.
        </p>

        <table class="ref">
          <tbody><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
          <tr><td><code>[Required]</code></td><td>Value must be provided (not null / not empty)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>[StringLength(n)]</code></td><td>Maximum (and optional minimum) string length</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>[Range(a,b)]</code></td><td>Numeric value must fall within a range</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>[EmailAddress]</code></td><td>Value must look like a valid email</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>[RegularExpression]</code></td><td>Value must match a regex pattern</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>[Compare]</code></td><td>Value must match another property (e.g. confirm password)</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>

        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">Product.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">Product</span>
{
    [<span class="hljs-meta">Required(ErrorMessage = <span class="hljs-string">"Product name is required."</span>)</span>]
    [<span class="hljs-meta">StringLength(100, MinimumLength = 3)</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Name { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">Range(0.01, 100000, ErrorMessage = <span class="hljs-string">"Price must be positive."</span>)</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">decimal</span> Price { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">EmailAddress</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> SupplierEmail { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">ProductsController.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpPost</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">Create</span>(<span class="hljs-params">Product product</span>)</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (!ModelState.IsValid)
        <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> BadRequest(ModelState); <span class="hljs-comment">// returns a 400 with all failed rules</span>

    _repo.Add(product);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> Ok(product);
}

<span class="hljs-comment">// Note: controllers marked [ApiController] check ModelState.IsValid</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// AUTOMATICALLY before the action even runs, and return 400 for you.</span></code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <b>Good to know</b>
          Data Annotations live on the model itself, so the same rules apply everywhere that model is bound —
          which is convenient, but also means the model can't easily have different rules for different
          scenarios (e.g. "required on create, optional on update").
        </div>

        <h3>Example: a fuller model with more built-in attributes</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">RegisterDto.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">RegisterDto</span>
{
    [<span class="hljs-meta">Required, StringLength(30, MinimumLength = 2)</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Username { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">Required, Phone</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> PhoneNumber { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">Url</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>? Website { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">CreditCard</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>? CardNumber { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">Required, DataType(DataType.Password)</span>]
    [<span class="hljs-meta">RegularExpression(@<span class="hljs-string">"^(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*\d).{8,}$"</span>,
        ErrorMessage = <span class="hljs-string">"Password needs 8+ chars, one uppercase letter, one digit."</span>)</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Password { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">Compare(nameof(Password), ErrorMessage = <span class="hljs-string">"Passwords do not match."</span>)</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> ConfirmPassword { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: writing a custom Data Annotation</h3>
        <p>When the built-in attributes aren't enough, implement <code class="inline">ValidationAttribute</code>
        to create your own reusable rule:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">FutureDateAttribute.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">FutureDateAttribute</span> : <span class="hljs-title">ValidationAttribute</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">protected</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">override</span> ValidationResult? IsValid(<span class="hljs-built_in">object</span>? <span class="hljs-keyword">value</span>, ValidationContext context)
    {
        <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (<span class="hljs-keyword">value</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">is</span> DateTime date &amp;&amp; date &lt;= DateTime.Today)
            <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> ValidationResult(<span class="hljs-string">"Date must be in the future."</span>);

        <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> ValidationResult.Success;
    }
}

<span class="hljs-comment">// Usage on a model:</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">Booking</span>
{
    [<span class="hljs-meta">FutureDate</span>]
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> DateTime CheckInDate { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== 9. FLUENT VALIDATION INTRO ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="fluent-intro">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">9</span>Validation</div>
        <h2>Intro: Fluent API Validations</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>FluentValidation</strong> is a popular open-source library that moves validation rules out of
          the model and into dedicated <em>validator</em> classes, written using a fluent, chainable C# syntax.
          Instead of decorating properties with attributes, you describe rules in code — which makes complex,
          conditional, or reusable validation logic far easier to express and test.
        </p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">ProductValidator.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">ProductValidator</span> : <span class="hljs-title">AbstractValidator</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title">Product</span>&gt;
{
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-title">ProductValidator</span>()</span>
    {
        RuleFor(p =&gt; p.Name)
            .NotEmpty().WithMessage(<span class="hljs-string">"Product name is required."</span>)
            .Length(<span class="hljs-number">3</span>, <span class="hljs-number">100</span>);

        RuleFor(p =&gt; p.Price)
            .GreaterThan(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>).WithMessage(<span class="hljs-string">"Price must be positive."</span>);

        RuleFor(p =&gt; p.SupplierEmail)
            .EmailAddress()
            .When(p =&gt; !<span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>.IsNullOrEmpty(p.SupplierEmail));
    }
}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">Program.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes">builder.Services.AddValidatorsFromAssemblyContaining&lt;ProductValidator&gt;();
builder.Services.AddFluentValidationAutoValidation();</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>
          Once registered, FluentValidation plugs into the same model-validation pipeline as Data Annotations —
          a client posting an invalid <code class="inline">Product</code> still gets a 400 response with clear
          error messages, but the rules themselves now live in <code class="inline">ProductValidator</code>,
          completely separate from the <code class="inline">Product</code> class.
        </p>

        <h3>Example: running a validator manually (outside MVC)</h3>
        <p>Because a validator is just a plain class, you can call it directly — in a console app, a background
        job, or a unit test — with no controller or HTTP pipeline involved at all:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">Program.cs (console)</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> validator = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> ProductValidator();
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> product = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> Product { Name = <span class="hljs-string">"Mu"</span>, Price = <span class="hljs-number">-5</span> };

ValidationResult result = validator.Validate(product);

<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (!result.IsValid)
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">foreach</span> (<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> failure <span class="hljs-keyword">in</span> result.Errors)
        Console.WriteLine(<span class="hljs-string">$"<span class="hljs-subst">{failure.PropertyName}</span>: <span class="hljs-subst">{failure.ErrorMessage}</span>"</span>);
}
<span class="hljs-comment">// Output:</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// Name: The length of 'Name' must be at least 3 characters.</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// Price: 'Price' must be greater than '0'.</span></code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: unit testing a validator</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">ProductValidatorTests.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">ProductValidatorTests</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">readonly</span> ProductValidator _validator = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span>();

    [<span class="hljs-meta">Fact</span>]
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">void</span> <span class="hljs-title">Fails_when_name_is_too_short</span>()</span>
    {
        <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> result = _validator.TestValidate(<span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> Product { Name = <span class="hljs-string">"Mu"</span>, Price = <span class="hljs-number">10</span> });
        result.ShouldHaveValidationErrorFor(p =&gt; p.Name);
    }

    [<span class="hljs-meta">Fact</span>]
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">void</span> <span class="hljs-title">Passes_for_a_valid_product</span>()</span>
    {
        <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> result = _validator.TestValidate(<span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> Product { Name = <span class="hljs-string">"Mug"</span>, Price = <span class="hljs-number">10</span> });
        result.ShouldNotHaveAnyValidationErrors();
    }
}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== 10. FLUENT VALIDATION TYPES ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="fluent-types">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">10</span>Validation</div>
        <h2>Fluent API Validation Types</h2>
        <p>FluentValidation ships a rich set of built-in validators, all chained onto <code class="inline">RuleFor</code>.</p>

        <table class="ref">
          <tbody><tr><th>Validator</th><th>Checks</th></tr>
          <tr><td><code>NotEmpty()</code></td><td>Not null, not empty string, not default value</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>NotNull()</code></td><td>Value is not null</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Length(min, max)</code></td><td>String length falls within a range</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>EmailAddress()</code></td><td>Value is a syntactically valid email</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Matches(regex)</code></td><td>String matches a regular expression</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GreaterThan() / LessThan()</code></td><td>Numeric/date comparisons</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>InclusiveBetween(a, b)</code></td><td>Value falls within an inclusive range</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Must(predicate)</code></td><td>Custom logic — any condition you can express as a function</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>When() / Unless()</code></td><td>Applies a rule only under a given condition</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>SetValidator()</code></td><td>Delegates to another validator for a nested/child object</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>

        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">OrderValidator.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">OrderValidator</span> : <span class="hljs-title">AbstractValidator</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title">Order</span>&gt;
{
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-title">OrderValidator</span>()</span>
    {
        RuleFor(o =&gt; o.Quantity)
            .InclusiveBetween(<span class="hljs-number">1</span>, <span class="hljs-number">100</span>);

        RuleFor(o =&gt; o.Sku)
            .Matches(<span class="hljs-string">@"^[A-Z]{3}\d{4}$"</span>)
            .WithMessage(<span class="hljs-string">"SKU must look like ABC1234."</span>);

        RuleFor(o =&gt; o.DeliveryDate)
            .GreaterThan(DateTime.Today)
            .When(o =&gt; o.IsExpressDelivery);

        RuleFor(o =&gt; o.Customer)
            .SetValidator(<span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> CustomerValidator());   <span class="hljs-comment">// nested object</span>

        RuleFor(o =&gt; o)
            .Must(o =&gt; o.Total == o.Quantity * o.UnitPrice)
            .WithMessage(<span class="hljs-string">"Total does not match quantity × unit price."</span>);
    }
}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="badge-row">
          <span class="badge">RuleFor()</span>
          <span class="badge">RuleForEach()</span>
          <span class="badge">When() / Unless()</span>
          <span class="badge">Must()</span>
          <span class="badge">WithMessage()</span>
          <span class="badge">SetValidator()</span>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: validating a collection with RuleForEach</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">CartValidator.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">CartValidator</span> : <span class="hljs-title">AbstractValidator</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title">Cart</span>&gt;
{
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-title">CartValidator</span>()</span>
    {
        RuleFor(c =&gt; c.Items).NotEmpty().WithMessage(<span class="hljs-string">"Cart cannot be empty."</span>);

        RuleForEach(c =&gt; c.Items).ChildRules(item =&gt;
        {
            item.RuleFor(i =&gt; i.Quantity).GreaterThan(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>);
            item.RuleFor(i =&gt; i.ProductId).NotEmpty();
        });
    }
}
<span class="hljs-comment">// Every item in c.Items is validated individually — errors report</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// which index in the collection failed, e.g. "Items[2].Quantity".</span></code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: a fully custom validator with Must()</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">RegisterValidator.cs</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">RegisterValidator</span> : <span class="hljs-title">AbstractValidator</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title">RegisterDto</span>&gt;
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">readonly</span> IUserRepository _users;

    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-title">RegisterValidator</span>(<span class="hljs-params">IUserRepository users</span>)</span>
    {
        _users = users;

        RuleFor(r =&gt; r.Email)
            .NotEmpty()
            .EmailAddress()
            .MustAsync(<span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> (email, cancellation) =&gt;
                !<span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> _users.EmailExistsAsync(email))
            .WithMessage(<span class="hljs-string">"An account with this email already exists."</span>);

        RuleFor(r =&gt; r.Password)
            .MinimumLength(<span class="hljs-number">8</span>)
            .Must(p =&gt; p.Any(<span class="hljs-built_in">char</span>.IsDigit))
            .WithMessage(<span class="hljs-string">"Password must contain at least one digit."</span>);
    }
}
<span class="hljs-comment">// Shows validation reaching into a database (MustAsync) —</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// something Data Annotations cannot do at all.</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== 11. ADVANTAGES OF FLUENT VALIDATION ===================== -->
      <section class="topic" id="fluent-advantages">
        <div class="topic-eyebrow"><span class="num">11</span>Validation</div>
        <h2>Advantages of Fluent Validation</h2>
        <p>Compared to Data Annotations, FluentValidation trades a little extra setup for a lot more flexibility:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Separation of concerns</strong> — validation logic lives outside the model, so the model
          stays a plain data class with no framework-specific attributes cluttering it.</li>
          <li><strong>Testability</strong> — a validator is just a class; you can unit test it directly by calling
          <code class="inline">Validate()</code>, without spinning up MVC or ModelState at all.</li>
          <li><strong>Complex &amp; conditional rules</strong> — <code class="inline">When()</code>, <code class="inline">Unless()</code>,
          and <code class="inline">Must()</code> express rules that would be awkward or impossible with attributes alone,
          like "delivery date is required only for express orders."</li>
          <li><strong>Reusable, composable validators</strong> — one validator can call another via
          <code class="inline">SetValidator()</code> for nested objects, and validators can be reused across DTOs
          that share shape.</li>
          <li><strong>Multiple validators per type</strong> — you can define a "create" validator and a separate
          "update" validator for the same model, something attributes on the model can't express.</li>
          <li><strong>Cleaner error messages</strong> — messages are defined alongside logic in fluent, readable
          chains, and can reference other properties or computed context easily.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div class="compare-card a">
            <h4><span class="chip"></span>Choose Data Annotations when…</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Rules are simple and apply everywhere the model is used</li>
              <li>You want validation visible at a glance on the model itself</li>
              <li>The project is small and doesn't need much conditional logic</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="compare-card b">
            <h4><span class="chip"></span>Choose Fluent Validation when…</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Rules are complex, conditional, or differ by scenario</li>
              <li>You want validators to be independently unit-testable</li>
              <li>Models are shared across layers and shouldn't carry validation attributes</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <b>Takeaway for the course</b>
          Data Annotations are the fast, built-in default — great for learning and for straightforward models.
          FluentValidation is what most production teams reach for once validation logic grows past what a
          handful of attributes can cleanly express.
        </div>

        <h3>Example: the problem Data Annotations can't solve</h3>
        <p>Imagine a <code class="inline">Product</code> where <code class="inline">Sku</code> is required on
        <em>create</em> but read-only (and irrelevant to validate) on <em>update</em>. One model, one set of
        attributes, can't express two different rule sets:</p>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">the problem</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">Product</span>
{
    [<span class="hljs-meta">Required</span>]     <span class="hljs-comment">// always required — even during an update where it's not sent</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> Sku { <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span>; <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span>; }
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: FluentValidation's fix — separate validators per scenario</h3>
        <div class="code-card">
          <div class="code-head"><span class="fname">the fix</span><div class="dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div>
          <pre><code class="language-csharp hljs" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">CreateProductValidator</span> : <span class="hljs-title">AbstractValidator</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title">Product</span>&gt;
{
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-title">CreateProductValidator</span>()</span>
    {
        RuleFor(p =&gt; p.Sku).NotEmpty();      <span class="hljs-comment">// required only when creating</span>
        RuleFor(p =&gt; p.Name).NotEmpty();
    }
}

<span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">UpdateProductValidator</span> : <span class="hljs-title">AbstractValidator</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title">Product</span>&gt;
{
    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-title">UpdateProductValidator</span>()</span>
    {
        RuleFor(p =&gt; p.Name).NotEmpty();     <span class="hljs-comment">// Sku isn't validated here at all</span>
    }
}

<span class="hljs-comment">// The controller resolves whichever validator fits the action being called:</span>
[<span class="hljs-meta">HttpPost</span>]
<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> IActionResult <span class="hljs-title">Create</span>(<span class="hljs-params">Product product, [FromServices] CreateProductValidator v</span>)</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> result = v.Validate(product);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> result.IsValid ? Ok(product) : BadRequest(result.Errors);
}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

